Found by an independent storage-layer audit, then reproduced a second time from
scratch by the orchestrating agent. Root cause of several other findings.

## Mechanism

Three decisions in `lib/App/karr/Git.pm` combine into silent data loss:

- `write_ref` (`Git.pm:237`) creates commits with `parents => []`, so **every**
  ref update is non-fast-forward by construction.
- `push` (`Git.pm:346`) uses the forced refspec `+refs/karr/*:refs/karr/*`.
- `pull` (`Git.pm:369`, `:375`) uses the **non-forced** `refs/karr/*:refs/karr/*`.

libgit2 declines the non-ff update on fetch, reports **no error**, `last_error`
stays unset, and `pull` returns 1. So `karr sync` prints "Done.", exits 0, and
the local ref is silently unchanged. A ref that does not exist locally yet is
fetched fine — which is why this hides: the first sync of a task always works,
only updates are lost.

## Reproduction (verified twice, independently)

    git init -q --bare origin.git
    # clone a and b, both with the board synced, task 1 exists on both

    karr --dir a edit 1 -a "note from A"    # pushes A's version
    karr --dir b sync --pull                # "Done.", exit 0
    karr --dir b show 1                     # "note from A" is MISSING
    karr --dir b edit 1 -a "note from B"    # force-pushes B's stale version

Origin's task 1 afterwards contains `hello / note from B`. **"note from A" is
gone from the shared board.** No warning anywhere, exit 0 on every step.

## Why this is critical

This is unattended, silent data loss in exactly the multi-agent workflow karr
exists for, and karr-foundation runs parallel agents against shared boards. Any
two agents touching the same task lose one side's work; the loser is never told.

It is also the keystone for other findings: it is the direct cause of the
CLI-fallback lockout, the realistic trigger for the open3 stderr deadlock (a
diverged board rejects every ref, which is what produces the >64 KiB of stderr),
and the reason stale-clone deletes resurrect.

## Fix direction

Forcing the fetch refspec (`+refs/karr/*:refs/karr/*`) makes pull match push and
converts this into last-writer-wins without the stale read — which is the
semantics the parentless-commit design already implies everywhere else. That is
the minimal fix and it should come with a regression test using two clones.

Worth deciding separately, and probably its own ticket: whether last-writer-wins
is good enough for the multi-agent case at all, or whether the write path needs
compare-and-swap. This ticket is only about pull not silently doing nothing.
